NONE=' The closing statement uses no property of the chain, so it holds on all 21 networks alike.'
# ---------------- OpenZeppelin
c("OpenZeppelin audit, C-01: an ERC-20 representation of the native currency lets one payment be credited twice, once as msg.value under address(0) and once as a balance increase under the token, which drains native pools (worked case Celo, with Polygon as a network where deltas can be inflated).",
  "Closed in the source. Hazard in one sentence: sync the token face, pay with settle and value, then settle the face for the same inflow. Closing statements: a settle with value reverts while any token is synced (src/PoolManager.sol:356); a native credit is taken from msg.value only when no currency is synced (353-354); every write of a nonzero synced currency also writes the reserves (285-286, src/libraries/CurrencyReserves.sol:27-32), so a face synced after a native payment starts from the higher balance; a token settle clears the slot (361); the contract has no receive or fallback function and only settle and settleFor are payable (300-307). The report says the fix makes the next settle after a sync settle that currency or revert. The source is looser in one way: sync(address(0)) clears the slot without a settle (283, documented at src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:166-171). I walked the orders that this allows and each inflow is still credited under one id only, because the stale reserves are overwritten by the next token sync. Reliance: A1 (the synced currency lives in transient storage for one transaction and is rolled back on revert), A2 and A3 (native value reaches the PoolManager through settle, and anything forced in is credited once under one id). Per network: an address that reports the PoolManager's native balance exists on Celo (CELO token, transfer works), Arc (0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000, six decimals, transfer works), Polygon (0x1010, transfer of 1 without value returned false), Optimism and Zora (LegacyERC20ETH, transfer disabled). My query found no such answer from these candidates on the other 16 networks, and the ledger holds a face search for each of them. On all 21 deployed PoolManagers my walk with a stub token that returns the native balance of its argument gave: sync while locked succeeded, settle with 1000 wei reverted with NonzeroNativeValue, a zero value settle paid 0 and cleared the slot, settle with 1000 wei then credited 1000, a new sync and settle of the face paid 0, and a native take of 1000 closed the unlock. The real faces on Celo, Polygon, Arc, Optimism and Zora gave the same seven answers. The walk shows the contracts deployed today. The checkout was not compiled.",
  OZ+" pages 10-11; src/PoolManager.sol:279-288, 300-307, 349-365; src/libraries/CurrencyReserves.sol:21-32; eth_call with state overrides on the 21 rpc URLs of state/networks.json on 2026-11-23 (scripts walk.py and faces.py, results raw/walk.json and raw/faces.json in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work); cleared items of core-code-accounting (cycle 1), core-code-invariants-settlement (cycle 2) and core-code-loose-guards (cycle 6)",lens='L3')
c("OpenZeppelin audit, security model and trust assumptions (page 9): core has no slippage protection, non-standard and malicious tokens are not supported, malicious hooks can take from users of their pool, and anyone can create a pool with any token.",
  "These are stated limits and nothing in src closes them. What src provides is separation: pool state is stored per pool id (src/PoolManager.sol:93), deltas per caller and currency (src/libraries/CurrencyDelta.sol:10-41), hook deltas are booked to the hook address in the two currencies of its pool (PoolManager.sol:181, 224, 381-384). Reliance: A2 and A4 for tokens, A7 for hooks. The ledger records where these fail per network (rebasing and reward tokens F-012, F-041, F-066, F-067, F-091, tokens that move or burn the PoolManager balance F-085, F-086, F-090, F-099, issuer controls F-057, F-080). This entry adds no new case.",
  OZ+" page 9; src/PoolManager.sol:93, 181, 224, 381-384; src/libraries/CurrencyDelta.sol:10-41",lens='L3')
c("OpenZeppelin audit, M-01: assembly blocks marked memory-safe wrote past the scratch space or over the free memory pointer (hook return data, the TickMisaligned error, two argument custom errors, the ERC-20 transfer call).",
  "Closed. The hook return data is copied to memory at the free memory pointer, which is then advanced by the padded length (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:140-149). TickMisaligned and the two argument revert helpers build their data at the free memory pointer and revert at once (src/libraries/TickBitmap.sol:57-63, src/libraries/CustomRevert.sol:49-79, 83-118). The single argument helpers write 36 bytes into the 64 byte scratch space (CustomRevert.sol:14-46). The ERC-20 transfer writes its 68 bytes of calldata at the free memory pointer, reads the answer into the first scratch word and zeroes the three words it used (src/types/Currency.sol:55-80). Reliance: A10 only, that MSTORE, MLOAD, RETURNDATACOPY and CALL behave as on Ethereum, which cycle 1 tested on every network.",
  OZ+" page 12; src/libraries/Hooks.sol:131-155; src/libraries/TickBitmap.sol:47-75; src/libraries/CustomRevert.sol:14-118; src/types/Currency.sol:40-88")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, M-02: a malicious protocol fee controller could grief pool initialization by returning a large amount of data from the fee query that the PoolManager made with a gas limit.",
  "Closed by removal of the call. The source contains no call from the PoolManager to the controller: a search of src outside src/test finds no controllerGasLimit and no fee query, and the only external calls are the unlock callback, hook calls, the native payout, the ERC-20 transfer and balanceOf (src/PoolManager.sol:110, src/libraries/Hooks.sol:134, src/types/Currency.sol:48, 73, 94, 102). The controller now pushes a fee with setProtocolFee (src/ProtocolFees.sol:35-41), and a new pool starts with protocol fee 0 (src/libraries/Pool.sol:105-106)."+NONE,
  OZ+" pages 12-13; src/ProtocolFees.sol:29-57; src/PoolManager.sol:117-142; src/libraries/Pool.sol:100-107")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, L-01: hook calldata was built with abi.encodeWithSelector, which does not check argument types.",
  "Closed. All ten hook calls use abi.encodeCall with the IHooks function (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:180, 190, 202, 204, 225, 237, 256, 300, 323, 333). I recomputed the ten selectors and none equals a selector of an external PoolManager function, nor do transfer(address,uint256), balanceOf(address) and unlockCallback(bytes)."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 14; src/libraries/Hooks.sol:178-335; script pm_selectors.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, L-02: four require statements carry no error message (BitMath and FullMath). Acknowledged, not resolved.",
  "Unchanged and without effect on funds. The four statements are at src/libraries/BitMath.sol:13 and 32 and src/libraries/FullMath.sol:30 and 113. They revert with empty data. A caller sees no reason string, and no state is left behind."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 15; src/libraries/BitMath.sol:13, 32; src/libraries/FullMath.sol:30, 113")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, L-03: fromId turns a uint256 into a currency and drops the upper 12 bytes, so mint and burn could act on an id that differs from the currency. The report says the team added a mask to fromId.",
  "Closed. fromId casts the id to uint160 (src/types/Currency.sol:114-118), which drops the upper 96 bits, and mint and burn pass currency.toId() to the claim ledger and not the raw argument (src/PoolManager.sol:324-327, 333-335). A raw id with upper bits therefore debits or credits the same currency and the same claim id as the clean one, and no claim can exist under an id with upper bits, because _mint is reached only from line 327. The direct ERC-6909 functions take raw ids (src/ERC6909.sol:25-56) and find a zero balance under any id with upper bits."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 15; src/types/Currency.sol:110-118; src/PoolManager.sol:322-336; src/ERC6909.sol:25-56, 79-89")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-01: floating and mixed Solidity pragmas.",
  "Addressed as the report describes. PoolManager.sol is pinned to 0.8.26 (src/PoolManager.sol:2), nine files that use transient storage say ^0.8.24 and 36 files say ^0.8.0. foundry.toml fixes solc 0.8.26, evm_version cancun and via_ir for the build. Reliance: A10, that the target network runs the cancun opcodes, which holds on the 21 networks per the ledger.",
  OZ+" page 16; src/PoolManager.sol:2; foundry.toml:1-10")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-02: BalanceDelta and BeforeSwapDelta assembly blocks lacked the memory-safe annotation.",
  "Addressed. Every assembly block in src/types/BalanceDelta.sol (lines 15, 23, 37, 62, 68) and src/types/BeforeSwapDelta.sol (lines 13, 26, 34) carries the annotation and touches only stack values. No effect on funds."+NONE,
  OZ+" pages 16-17; src/types/BalanceDelta.sol:14-71; src/types/BeforeSwapDelta.sol:9-37")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-03: ERC-6909 transferFrom skips the operator and allowance checks when the sender is the caller, which differed from the specification text.",
  "Unchanged by design. The report says the specification was adjusted. The check is at src/ERC6909.sol:36 and the same rule is used for burns at src/ERC6909Claims.sol:15. A holder moving its own claims needs no approval and no other account gains a right from this."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 17; src/ERC6909.sol:35-48; src/ERC6909Claims.sol:13-22")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-04: code clarity in Pool (negated conditions, compound operators). Partially resolved.",
  "No effect on funds. The compound operators are in place (src/libraries/Pool.sol:373-381). I read both branches of each condition in the swap loop for their sign: exact output subtracts amountOut from the remainder and subtracts amountIn plus fee from the calculated amount, exact input adds amountIn plus fee to the negative remainder and adds amountOut (Pool.sol:370-382)."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 17; src/libraries/Pool.sol:370-382, 451-462")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-05 and N-06: missing parameter names in IProtocolFees, and parameter names that differed between interfaces and implementations.",
  "Addressed. IProtocolFees names every parameter (src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:28-47). IExtsload uses slot, startSlot and slots as the implementation does (src/interfaces/IExtsload.sol:9-20, src/Extsload.sol:10-42), setOperator names its first parameter operator (src/interfaces/external/IERC6909Claims.sol:62-65), and settle takes no argument in both places (src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:183, src/PoolManager.sol:300). No effect on funds."+NONE,
  OZ+" pages 17-18; the files named")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-07, N-08 and N-09: unused named return variables, unused imports and the unused error TickNotInitialized.",
  "Addressed. The functions the report lists return unnamed values or use their named value (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:159, src/libraries/SqrtPriceMath.sol:34, 89, 132, 161, 191, 237, 264, 281). The listed imports are gone (src/PoolManager.sol:20, src/libraries/StateLibrary.sol:4-6, src/libraries/TransientStateLibrary.sol:4-8, src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:4-13), and a search of src finds no TickNotInitialized. No effect on funds."+NONE,
  OZ+" pages 18-20; the files named")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-10: three unexplained literal numbers in TickMath. Acknowledged, not resolved.",
  "Unchanged, and I checked the numbers by computation. A Python model built from the constants as they stand in src/libraries/TickMath.sol gives getSqrtPriceAtTick(MIN_TICK) equal to MIN_SQRT_PRICE and getSqrtPriceAtTick(MAX_TICK) equal to MAX_SQRT_PRICE, a relative error below 2.2 times 10^-10 against 1.0001^(tick/2) on 3,045 ticks, and the round trip getTickAtSqrtPrice(getSqrtPriceAtTick(t)) equal to t with the price one unit lower mapping to t - 1, which exercises the literals at lines 225 to 233. The BitMath lookup constants returned the right bit index for every single bit input and for 200,000 random inputs. Reliance: A10, that MUL, SHR, SAR, BYTE and SIGNEXTEND behave as on Ethereum, which cycle 1 tested on every network.",
  OZ+" page 20; src/libraries/TickMath.sol:57-237; src/libraries/BitMath.sol:12-48; script tickmath.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-11: constants without explicit visibility in Lock, NonzeroDeltaCount and the reserves library.",
  "Addressed in two of three places and without effect in the third. IS_UNLOCKED_SLOT and NONZERO_DELTA_COUNT_SLOT are declared internal (src/libraries/Lock.sol:8, src/libraries/NonzeroDeltaCount.sol:9-10). RESERVES_OF_SLOT and CURRENCY_SLOT carry no visibility word (src/libraries/CurrencyReserves.sol:11, 13), and a library constant without one is internal. I recomputed all four slot values from their stated preimages and they match."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 20; src/libraries/Lock.sol:8; src/libraries/NonzeroDeltaCount.sol:9-10; src/libraries/CurrencyReserves.sol:10-13; script consts.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-12 and N-13: unnamed mapping parameters in ERC6909, and no indexed parameter in ProtocolFeeControllerUpdated.",
  "Addressed. The three mappings name their parameters (src/ERC6909.sol:15-19) and the event indexes the controller (src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:20). The storage layout that StateLibrary and extsload readers rely on is not changed by parameter names. No effect on funds."+NONE,
  OZ+" page 21; src/ERC6909.sol:15-19; src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:20")
c("OpenZeppelin audit, N-14, N-15 and N-16: missing and incomplete docstrings, and no security contact.",
  "Documentation items with no effect on funds. The interfaces now document parameters and return values (src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:109-216, src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:25-51) and SECURITY.md exists at the repository root. One docstring disagrees with the source: IProtocolFees.sol:40 says collectProtocolFees reverts if the contract is unlocked, and the function reads no lock (src/ProtocolFees.sol:44-57). That disagreement is in the first finding of this file and in F-006."+NONE,
  OZ+" pages 21-24; src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:109-216; src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:25-51; SECURITY.md")
# ---------------- Trail of Bits
c("Trail of Bits audit, finding 1: a strict equality in the 100 percent fee check would let a swap fee above 100 percent pass.",
  "Closed. The check is swapFee >= SwapMath.MAX_SWAP_FEE and rejects exact output swaps (src/libraries/Pool.sol:311-316). I also checked that the fee cannot exceed the maximum at all: a static fee is validated at initialize (src/libraries/LPFeeLibrary.sol:51-56, src/PoolManager.sol:128), a dynamic fee at updateDynamicLPFee (PoolManager.sol:343), an override at Pool.sol:303-305, each against 1,000,000, the protocol fee against 1000 per direction (src/libraries/ProtocolFeeLibrary.sol:25-32), and the combined fee is protocolFee + lpFee - floor(protocolFee * lpFee / 1,000,000) (lines 38-46). A Python run over every protocol fee from 0 to 1000 and 3,200 LP fees each, the top 1,000 values included, gave a maximum of exactly 1,000,000 and never less than the protocol fee, which also keeps the unchecked subtraction at Pool.sol:395 from wrapping."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed pages 29-30 and 61; src/libraries/Pool.sol:300-316, 385-398; src/libraries/ProtocolFeeLibrary.sol:25-46; script props.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("Trail of Bits audit, finding 2: the swap fee was compared with the LP fee maximum instead of a swap fee maximum.",
  "Closed. The comparison uses SwapMath.MAX_SWAP_FEE (src/libraries/Pool.sol:311), the constant is 1e6 (src/libraries/SwapMath.sol:12) and computeSwapStep uses the same constant in all four places (lines 65, 72, 74, 104). LPFeeLibrary.MAX_LP_FEE keeps its own role as the bound on the LP fee (src/libraries/LPFeeLibrary.sol:25, 37-39)."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed pages 31-32 and 61-62; src/libraries/Pool.sol:311; src/libraries/SwapMath.sol:12, 65-104")
c("Trail of Bits audit, finding 4: calculateSwapFee cleared the protocol fee with a 16 bit mask although the value has 12 bits.",
  "Closed. The mask is 0xfff (src/libraries/ProtocolFeeLibrary.sol:41) and the LP fee mask is 0xffffff (line 42). The value passed in comes from getZeroForOneFee or getOneForZeroFee, which already return 12 bits (lines 17-23, src/libraries/Pool.sol:286-287, 307). isValidProtocolFee matches its high level statement on all 16,777,216 uint24 inputs in my Python check."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed pages 35-36 and 62; src/libraries/ProtocolFeeLibrary.sol:17-46; script props.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("Trail of Bits audit, finding 5: several operations emit no event (updateDynamicLPFee, donate, settle, take), and Initialize lacked the price. Partially resolved.",
  "Matches the fix review and has no effect on funds. Initialize carries sqrtPriceX96 and tick (src/PoolManager.sol:139) and donate emits Donate (line 273). updateDynamicLPFee, sync, settle, take, clear and collectProtocolFees emit nothing (lines 279-319, 339-346, src/ProtocolFees.sol:44-57). The effect is on monitoring: a fee change by a hook and a protocol fee withdrawal leave no log of the PoolManager, so watchers on every network must read state or token Transfer logs."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed pages 37-38 and 62; src/PoolManager.sol:139, 175, 241-250, 273, 339-346; src/ProtocolFees.sol:29-57",lens='L5')
c("Trail of Bits audit, appendix C, item 1: ownership of the PoolManager moves in one step, so a wrong address loses the controller setter.",
  "Unchanged. Owned.transferOwnership writes the new owner at once (lib/solmate/src/auth/Owned.sol:39-43) and the owner's one power is setProtocolFeeController (src/ProtocolFees.sol:29-32). This needs the owner and is outside the unprivileged scope. Reliance: A12. Who can act as owner on each network is in the ledger (F-009, F-010, F-016, F-022, F-026, F-029, F-034).",
  TOB+" printed page 46; lib/solmate/src/auth/Owned.sol:17-43; src/ProtocolFees.sol:29-32",lens='L5')
c("Trail of Bits audit, appendix C, items 2 to 6: a local variable named state in Pool.modifyLiquidity, the NonZeroDeltaCount slot preimage, comments in the Currency transfer function, the OpenZeppelin library version, and documentation that named the high bits of the hook address.",
  "No effect on funds. The local variable is still called state (src/libraries/Pool.sol:156). The library is named NonzeroDeltaCount and its slot is keccak256 of that string minus 1, which I recomputed (src/libraries/NonzeroDeltaCount.sol:7-10). The comments in the transfer block describe the code they sit on (src/types/Currency.sol:56-79). src imports one external file, solmate Owned (src/ProtocolFees.sol:9), and no OpenZeppelin contract. The source uses the low 14 bits of the hook address and says so (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:15-18, 27-47)."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed page 46; the files named; script consts.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("Trail of Bits audit, appendix C, item 7: take and collectProtocolFees do not reject the zero address as recipient, which can lose tokens.",
  "Unchanged. Neither function checks the recipient (src/PoolManager.sol:291-297, src/ProtocolFees.sol:44-57). The caller of take is debited the full amount before the transfer (line 294), so a payout to the zero address or to any address that swallows it costs the caller and no other account. Reliance: A3 and A4, what a native or token transfer to a given recipient does on each network. The ledger records the recipients that strand a payout per network (F-021, F-033, F-047, F-048, F-049) and the tokens whose transfer costs the PoolManager more than the debit (F-092).",
  TOB+" printed page 46; src/PoolManager.sol:291-297; src/ProtocolFees.sol:44-57; src/types/Currency.sol:40-88",lens='L4')
c("Trail of Bits audit, settlement invariants UNI-SETTLE-1 to UNI-SETTLE-13: a settle never raises what the user is owed beyond the amount paid, the amount paid equals what reached the singleton, and burn, mint and clear move the delta and the claim balance by exactly the stated amount.",
  "The source has these properties for a token whose balance changes only by transfers. settle credits msg.value or the checked balance difference and nothing else (src/PoolManager.sol:349-365). burn adds the amount to the caller's delta and removes it from the claim balance of from after the operator or allowance check (332-336, src/ERC6909Claims.sol:13-22, src/ERC6909.sol:85-89). mint does the reverse (322-329). clear accepts only the exact positive delta and sets it to zero (310-319). Every amount passes toInt128, which rejects 2^127 and above (src/libraries/SafeCast.sol:56-59). Reliance: UNI-SETTLE-3 rests on A2 and A3. It fails by design for a token whose balanceOf rises without a transfer from the settler, and the ledger lists those per network (F-041, F-066, F-067, F-079, F-081, F-091).",
  TOB+" printed pages 19-20; src/PoolManager.sol:300-336, 349-378; src/ERC6909Claims.sol:13-22",lens='L3')
c("Trail of Bits audit, take invariants UNI-TAKE-1 to UNI-TAKE-3: after a take the caller's delta falls by the amount, the recipient's balance rises by it and the singleton's balance falls by it.",
  "UNI-TAKE-1 holds from the source alone: the debit is written before the transfer and the call reverts as a whole if the transfer fails (src/PoolManager.sol:291-297, src/types/Currency.sol:44-53, 81-86). UNI-TAKE-2 and UNI-TAKE-3 rest on A3 and A4 and the source cannot enforce them: the token branch accepts an empty answer or a 32 byte 1 and makes no code size check (Currency.sol:64-74), so a take of an address without code moves nothing, and a token may deliver less to the recipient or charge the sender more. In each case the caller is debited the full amount, so the shortfall falls on the caller, except where a token charges the PoolManager on top, which the ledger holds as F-092 for Ethereum, Arc and BNB Smart Chain. Unit theme-transfer-side-effects of this cycle searches for further tokens of that kind.",
  TOB+" printed page 21; src/PoolManager.sol:291-297; src/types/Currency.sol:40-88",lens='L3')
c("Trail of Bits audit, end to end and inter-action invariants UNI-E2E-1 to UNI-E2E-3 and UNI-ACTION-1 to UNI-ACTION-5: no delta is left when the manager locks again, a round trip swap gives no gain, and what is owed to an actor or to the protocol never exceeds the singleton's balance.",
  "The source enforces the first directly: unlock reverts unless the count of nonzero deltas is zero and then closes the lock (src/PoolManager.sol:112-113), and the count moves only in _accountDelta when a delta enters or leaves zero (368-378). The others follow from the rounding directions, which I read again: amounts owed by the caller round up and amounts paid to the caller round down in liquidity changes (src/libraries/SqrtPriceMath.sol:261-288) and in each swap step (src/libraries/SwapMath.sol:63-105), and protocol fees are carved out of the fee the swapper already owes (src/libraries/Pool.sol:385-398). Earlier cycles replayed these with models and with the repository tests. Reliance: A2 for the balance side and A1 for the delta count. UNI-ACTION-6 is in the first finding of this file.",
  TOB+" printed pages 21-22; src/PoolManager.sol:104-114, 368-384; src/libraries/SqrtPriceMath.sol:261-288; src/libraries/Pool.sol:385-398")
c("Trail of Bits audit, pool invariants UNI-INIT-1 to 10, UNI-SWAP-1 to 24, UNI-DONATE-1 to 9 and UNI-MODLIQ-1 to 11, read for the statements that guard funds: bounds on price and tick, zero amount swaps, price limits, donations to an empty pool and fee credits.",
  "The guarding statements are present. initialize rejects tick spacing outside 1 to 32767, unordered or equal currencies, an invalid hook address, a fee above the maximum and a price outside the range (src/PoolManager.sol:119-128, src/libraries/TickMath.sol:127-129), and a second initialize reverts (src/libraries/Pool.sol:101). swap rejects a zero amount (PoolManager.sol:193) and a price limit on the wrong side of the price or at or beyond the bounds (Pool.sol:322-338), and clamps the next tick to the tick range (351-356). donate reverts on zero liquidity (Pool.sol:468). Fee credits come from fee growth differences times the position's liquidity before the change (src/libraries/Position.sol:82-97). I found no property of these lists that the text contradicts."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed pages 16-21; src/PoolManager.sol:117-142, 187-227; src/libraries/Pool.sol:100-107, 279-480; src/libraries/Position.sol:76-102")
c("Trail of Bits audit, stateless and differential invariants UNI-PROTOFEE-1 and 2, UNI-LIQMATH-1 and 2 and UNI-LOWLEVEL-3 to 8: the assembly in ProtocolFeeLibrary, LiquidityMath, SwapMath.getSqrtPriceTarget, TickBitmap.compress and Pool.tickSpacingToMaxLiquidityPerTick equals its high level statement.",
  "Holds for the text as it stands. I transcribed each block to Python with EVM semantics for SDIV, SMOD, SLT and SIGNEXTEND and compared it with the Solidity statement in the comment beside it: isValidProtocolFee on all 2^24 inputs, calculateSwapFee on about 3.2 million pairs with a maximum of 1,000,000, addDelta on 200,000 random pairs and five edge pairs with a revert exactly when the sum leaves the uint128 range, getSqrtPriceTarget on 200,000 triples, compress on 300,000 pairs, and the per tick liquidity cap for spacings 1 to 2999 and 32767 (191757530477355301479181766273477 at spacing 1, the value of test/Tick.t.sol). Reliance: A10. The ledger holds the opcode tests on each network (cycle 1) and the critic's open note that Monad, Tempo and BNB Smart Chain run compiled or fused code paths that an init code probe does not reach.",
  TOB+" printed pages 22-24; src/libraries/ProtocolFeeLibrary.sol:25-46; src/libraries/LiquidityMath.sol:10-19; src/libraries/SwapMath.sol:20-37; src/libraries/TickBitmap.sol:16-29; src/libraries/Pool.sol:565-582; script props.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
c("Trail of Bits audit, static invariants (appendix E): functions with noSelfCall return nothing, every function that calls a hook is protected against a call by the hook itself, no PoolManager selector equals a hook selector, and only settle and settleFor are payable.",
  "All four hold in the text. noSelfCall sits on beforeInitialize, afterInitialize, beforeModifyLiquidity, beforeDonate and afterDonate, none of which returns a value (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:178, 185, 195-200, 318-320, 328-330). The three hook callers that return values compare msg.sender with the hook first (lines 217, 253, 293). I computed the selectors of the 33 external functions of the PoolManager and its parents and of the 13 calls it can send (ten hook functions, transfer, balanceOf, unlockCallback) and found no equal pair, so a pool whose hook or currency is the PoolManager itself reverts on every such call. settle and settleFor are the only payable functions (src/PoolManager.sol:300, 305) and there is no receive or fallback function."+NONE,
  TOB+" printed pages 24 and 56-60; src/libraries/Hooks.sol:170-335; src/PoolManager.sol:300-307; script pm_selectors.py in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-a.work")
json.dump(cl,open('part2.json','w'),indent=1); print(len(cl))
